EXP['tz5e-astana-kazakhstan-electric-dump-truck'] = '''
<h2>Winter Operations: The Fleet Manager&rsquo;s Protocol</h2>
<p>Fleets preparing for their first Kazakh winter with electric trucks should adopt the protocol our Central Asian deployments have refined, because winter success is operational discipline applied to sound engineering. October: the winter-readiness service &mdash; thermal-system verification, coolant specification check, cold-rated cable inspection, and the driver refresher on winter charge discipline. November through March: the daily rhythm changes in three ways &mdash; trucks plug in whenever parked (no exceptions, wired into depot layout so parking and charging are the same act), the midday charge becomes a scheduled dispatch event rather than an emergency response, and the morning pre-departure check adds the pack-temperature readout to the walkaround. The telematics layer supports all of it: the fleet manager&rsquo;s winter dashboard tracks pack temperature at departure, charge-session completion, and per-truck winter efficiency, flagging any truck whose thermal behaviour deviates from the fleet norm.</p>
<p>The workforce dimension matters as much as the technical one. Kazakh drivers are among the world&rsquo;s most winter-competent professionals, and their initial scepticism &mdash; earned through decades of diesel winter failures &mdash; converts quickly when the truck starts every -30&deg;C morning without the diesel&rsquo;s pre-heater ritual, glow-plug wait, or frozen-fuel anxiety. The conversion story we hear repeatedly: the driver who insisted on keeping &ldquo;his&rdquo; diesel for the coldest weeks is, by February, refusing to go back &mdash; the warm cab on arrival, the instant torque on ice, and the absence of cold-start mechanical sympathy win the argument. Fleets should plan for that trajectory: run the winter with both technologies available, let the drivers&rsquo; own experience settle the comparison, and watch the dispatch board reallocate itself toward the trucks that never have a cold morning.</p>
'''

EXP['tz8j-cycle-time-vs-diesel-mixer-comparison'] = '''
<h2>What the Dispatch Data Shows After Twelve Months</h2>
<p>Cycle-time parity is the claim; the accumulated fleet data is the proof, and it is worth describing what plants report after a year of mixed-fleet operation. Utilisation records show the electric mixers matching diesel availability within a percentage point &mdash; and the misses trace overwhelmingly to process learning (a missed charge slot, a dispatch habit carried over from diesel) rather than vehicle faults. Energy records confirm the 65-75% per-cycle saving holds across seasons and route mixes. Driver records show preference consolidating toward the electric trucks within a quarter &mdash; the queue comfort, the smooth low-speed control at the pour, and the reduced end-of-shift fatigue are cited consistently. And the maintenance records tell the structural story: the electric fleet&rsquo;s workshop hours per truck run at roughly half the diesel fleet&rsquo;s, concentrated in brakes, suspension and drum hardware rather than powertrain.</p>
<p>The plant manager&rsquo;s summary metric &mdash; cost per cubic metre delivered &mdash; integrates all of it, and the plants running both technologies report the gap stabilising at 20-30% in the electric fleet&rsquo;s favour after the learning quarter, widening as utilisation rises. For plants considering the transition, the operational evidence suggests a low-risk sequencing: convert the highest-utilisation trucks first (the two-shift workhorses where savings and data accumulate fastest), retain diesel for the irregular long-distance outliers during the transition, and expand as the charge infrastructure and driver base grow. The pour schedule &mdash; the metric this article opened with &mdash; survives the transition intact, and the margin structure of the plant improves with every truck converted. In ready-mix, that is the whole business.</p>
'''

EXP['ethiopia-ice-import-ban-electric-truck-opportunity'] = '''
<h2>The Risks, Stated Honestly &mdash; and the Mitigations</h2>
<p>A serious assessment of the Ethiopian opportunity must name the frictions, because fleets that plan for them succeed and fleets that discover them struggle. Grid reliability outside Addis Ababa is the first: the mitigation is architectural &mdash; depot buffering through the fleet&rsquo;s own batteries, solar augmentation, and charging schedules that treat grid outages as routine events rather than emergencies. Parts logistics through a single corridor (Djibouti-Modjo) is the second: the mitigation is the extended first-line parts kit and the module-stock planning described in our support structure, sized explicitly for the corridor&rsquo;s lead times. Technical-skills depth is the third: the mitigation is the training investment &mdash; HV certification for Ethiopian technicians, delivered in Addis, building the local capability that every early fleet both needs and, frankly, benefits from monopolising initially.</p>
<p>The counterweight is what the policy guarantees: demand. Ethiopia has legislated its vehicle parc toward electric at a pace no other market matches, and the fleets, assemblers and service providers who establish positions now are building in a market where the state has removed the demand-side uncertainty that haunts EV investment elsewhere. The first-mover advantages compound in exactly the sectors named above &mdash; urban distribution, dry-port logistics, construction, municipal fleets &mdash; where early references become the standard against which later entrants are judged. Ethiopia&rsquo;s policy was the world&rsquo;s boldest because its economics were the world&rsquo;s clearest: a country that imports fuel it cannot afford and exports power it cannot use will electrify its transport as surely as water finds its level. The only question the policy left open is who builds the fleets &mdash; and it is open now, not forever.</p>
'''

EXP['how-many-charging-stations-does-an-ev-truck-fleet-need'] = '''
<h2>The Five Sizing Mistakes to Avoid</h2>
<p>Experience across our deployed fleets surfaces five recurring infrastructure errors, all avoidable at the design stage. First, the one-per-truck error already described &mdash; sizing on simultaneity that never occurs. Second, under-sizing the switchboard: fleets install for the day-one fleet and pay twice when expansion requires re-digging &mdash; always build electrical capacity for the three-year fleet, even if chargers are added later. Third, ignoring the utility clock: grid connection lead times (8-20 weeks in most markets) exceed vehicle delivery times, so the application must be filed at truck order, not truck arrival. Fourth, skipping load management: unmanaged charging turns a 350 kVA site into an apparent 800 kVA requirement &mdash; the controller that sequences sessions is the cheapest capacity a fleet can buy. Fifth, forgetting the second charger&rsquo;s redundancy logic: a fleet dependent on a single DC charger has a single point of failure &mdash; two smaller units often beat one large one for the same money.</p>
<p>The positive counterpart is a sizing discipline that costs nothing: measure, model, then build. Measure the duty cycle with real telematics where a diesel fleet exists; model the parking windows and session schedule against candidate charger configurations; and only then sign the electrical works. Fleets that follow this sequence consistently land within 10% of their optimal infrastructure spend, while fleets that skip it reliably overspend by half or undersize and retrofit. The charging depot is a twenty-year asset serving successive truck generations; the hour spent sizing it correctly is the highest-return planning hour in the entire electrification project &mdash; and the ratios in this article are where that sizing starts, not where it ends.</p>
'''

EXP['how-long-does-it-take-to-charge-an-electric-truck'] = '''
<h2>Reading Charging Specs Like an Engineer</h2>
<p>Buyers comparing charging claims across suppliers should apply three tests, because the marketing conventions hide more than they reveal. Test one &mdash; window: any figure quoted without a state-of-charge window is meaningless; &ldquo;charges in one hour&rdquo; might mean 20-80% (the useful operational window) or 10-60% (a much smaller energy transfer). Test two &mdash; power versus delivery: a truck &ldquo;capable of 350 kW&rdquo; only charges at that rate where 350 kW chargers exist and the pack is warm and in the fast-charge window; the fleet&rsquo;s real charging time is set by the hardware it will actually use. Test three &mdash; the curve: ask for the charging curve (power versus state of charge), not the headline number; two trucks with identical &ldquo;45-minute&rdquo; claims can differ by 20% in energy delivered if one holds peak power deeper into the session. Liquid-cooled LFP packs with modern charge maps hold their rate well &mdash; which is precisely the specification question the curve answers.</p>
<p>The operational conclusion follows directly: plan the fleet&rsquo;s energy schedule on delivered energy per session, not on time per session. A 45-minute window that adds 130 km of loaded range is operationally excellent; the same window adding 60 km changes the shift plan. Our telematics platform reports exactly this &mdash; energy per session, per truck, per charger &mdash; and fleets quickly learn to schedule by the numbers rather than the clock. Charging time, in the end, is not the constraint newcomers fear: it is a plannable, measurable input to the dispatch day, sitting in windows the operation already owns. The trucks that charge &ldquo;slowly&rdquo; in the brochure and fully every night beat the trucks that charge &ldquo;fast&rdquo; against hardware that does not exist &mdash; and the fleet that understands that distinction has understood electric trucking.</p>
'''

for fname, section in EXP.items():
    path = os.path.join(ROOT, 'blog', fname + '.html')
    with open(path, 'r', encoding='utf-8') as fh:
        html = fh.read()
    if MARKER not in html:
        print('MARKER MISSING:', fname)
        continue
    html = html.replace(MARKER, section.strip() + '\n\n' + MARKER, 1)
    with open(path, 'w', encoding='utf-8') as fh:
        fh.write(html)
    words = len(re.sub(r'<[^>]+>', ' ', html).split())
    print('%-58s -> %5d words' % (fname, words))
print('expansion done:', len(EXP))
